  "cut_point": "Let the original number be $ x $. Since it is a three-digit number, $ x $ is between 100 and 999.\n\nSuppose we move the last digit (i.e., the digit 3) two places to the left, making it the new first digit. The new number becomes $ 100 \\times 3 + x - 3 = 300 + x - 3 = 300 - 3 + x = 297 + x $.",
  "reflection": {
    "verified_state": [
      "Let the original number be $ x $.",
      "$ x $ is a three-digit number, so $ 100 \\le x \\le 999 $.",
      "The last digit of $ x $ is 3."
    ],
    "error_step": "The new number becomes $ 100 \\times 3 + x - 3 = 300 + x - 3 = 300 - 3 + x = 297 + x $.",
    "error_reason": "This treats $x-3$ as the remaining two-digit prefix, but $x-3$ is actually 10 times that prefix. Removing the units digit requires dividing by 10, so the new number is $300+(x-3)/10$, not $300+x-3$.",
    "new_direction": "Form the new number as $300+(x-3)/10$ (or write $x=100a+10b+3$ and use $300+10a+b$), then set it equal to $3x+1$ and solve."
  }
